import"./chunk-2NFLSA4Y.js";var r={electricPower:{theory:{concept:"[[Electric power is the energy that an electric current delivers to an appliance each second: P = U\xB7I, measured in watts (1 W = 1 J/s).]] The energy an appliance uses depends on its power and on how long it works: E = P\xB7t. An electricity meter counts this energy in kilowatt-hours, and the energy multiplied by the price gives the bill. The larger the total power of the appliances working together, the larger the current in the wires \u2014 which is why a circuit breaker limits it."},section:{power:{title:"Power and current",text:"[[Power is the energy used each second: 1 watt = 1 joule per second.]] An appliance connected to the mains of voltage U and drawing a current I has the power P = U\xB7I, so I = P/U. A 60 W bulb at 220 V draws 0.27 A, a 2 kW kettle 9.1 A. The powers of all the appliances that work together add up, and so do their currents: they all flow through the same meter and the same breaker."},energy:{title:"Energy: power \xD7 time",text:"[[The energy used is E = P\xB7t.]] In joules this is a tiny unit for a home, so electricity is counted in kilowatt-hours: 1 kWh is the energy of 1 kW running for 1 hour, which is 1000 W \xB7 3600 s = 3.6 million joules. A 100 W TV used for 10 hours takes 1 kWh. Notice that a big power for a short time can use less energy than a small power for a long time: the kettle (2 kW) boils water in 3 minutes and uses 0.1 kWh, but a 100 W TV uses the same in an hour."},meter:{title:"The electricity meter",text:"The meter adds up the energy: its counter shows the kilowatt-hours used so far. In an old meter a disc turns faster the more power is used; a modern one counts electronically. The bill is the number of kWh times the price of one kWh (here the price is 0.30 \u20BE per kWh, and you can change it; the real price depends on the tariff). In the simulation the disc turns once per 0.5 kWh so that you can see it; a real meter turns far more often."},lamps:{title:"Two lamps, the same light",text:"A 60 W incandescent bulb and a 9 W LED lamp shine equally brightly (about 800 lumens). The bulb turns most of its energy into heat: only a few per cent become light. The LED turns much more into light, so it needs 6.7 times less power for the same light, and over the same hours it uses 6.7 times less energy and costs 6.7 times less. Switch on each in turn and compare the meter."},kettle:{title:"Heating water",text:"To heat a mass m of water by \u0394T we need the energy Q = m\xB7c\xB7\u0394T, with c = 4186 J/(kg\xB7K). For 1 kg from 20 \xB0C to 100 \xB0C: Q = 335 kJ. The element passes only part of the electric energy, \u03B7 (about 90 %), to the water, so the electric energy is E = Q/\u03B7 and the time is t = m\xB7c\xB7\u0394T/(\u03B7\xB7P): for a 2 kW kettle about 3 minutes. The thermostat switches the element off at 100 \xB0C. More water takes proportionally more time and energy; a worse kettle wastes more."},breaker:{title:"The circuit breaker",text:"The wires in the wall heat up as P = I\xB2R, so the current must not be too big. A circuit breaker (or a fuse) switches the circuit off when the current exceeds its rating I_max. At 220 V a 16 A breaker allows up to 220 \xB7 16 = 3520 W: the kettle and the heater together (3.5 kW) just fit, but one more lamp is too much and the breaker trips. After a trip the load must be reduced before the breaker is switched back on. That is why you must not plug too many powerful appliances into one circuit."},saving:{title:"Saving energy",text:"[[Energy is saved in two ways: by using less power (an LED instead of a bulb) and by using it for less time (switch off what you do not need).]] Appliances that run only part of the time, like a refrigerator whose compressor works about a third of the time, use less than their power suggests: 120 W for a third of the time averages 40 W. Look at the average power in the readings."},check:{title:"Check your understanding",text:"A 1500 W heater runs for 2 hours at 0.30 \u20BE per kWh. How much energy and what cost? (3 kWh, 0.90 \u20BE.) What current does it draw at 220 V? (6.8 A.) Why does the breaker trip when the kettle, the heater and a lamp work together? (The total current is over 16 A.) Which is cheaper over 10 hours, a 60 W bulb or a 9 W LED lamp? (The LED, by 6.7 times.) Test each answer in the simulation."}},formula:{power:"The power of an appliance on the mains",energy:"The energy used in a time t at constant power",kwh:"The kilowatt-hour: the unit on the bill",cost:"The cost of the electricity",heat:"The heat needed to warm water (m: mass, c = 4186 J/(kg\xB7K))",boil:"The time to heat the water with a kettle of power P and efficiency \u03B7",breaker:"The breaker trips when the current is above its rating"},variable:{P:"Power",U:"Mains voltage",I:"Current",E:"Energy used",t:"Time",eta:"Efficiency of the kettle"},assumption:{constant:"Every appliance takes a constant power, and the current is simply P/U (a resistive load); a real motor, TV or compressor changes its power, and the mains voltage is taken as constant.",breaker:"The breaker trips as soon as the current is above its rating (a real one allows a short, small overload), and it is switched back on when the load is small enough.",kettle:"The water in the kettle is at one temperature and loses no heat to the air; the efficiency of the element is constant.",scale:"Not to scale: the disc of the meter turns once per 0.5 kWh (a real one turns far more often) and the pictures of the appliances are only symbols. The time is sped up."},param:{led:"LED lamp (9 W)",bulb:"Incandescent bulb (60 W)",kettle:"Kettle (2000 W)",heater:"Electric heater (1500 W)",tv:"Television (100 W)",computer:"Computer (150 W)",fridge:"Refrigerator (120 W while running)",hairdryer:"Hair dryer (1800 W)",duration:"Length of the run",timeScale:"Speed of time (minutes per second)",water:"Water in the kettle",tariff:"Price of 1 kWh (\u20BE)",efficiency:"Efficiency of the kettle",voltage:"Mains voltage",breaker:"Rating of the breaker",timeStep:"Time step"},hint:{led:"Gives about the same light as the 60 W bulb: 800 lumens.",bulb:"Most of its energy becomes heat, not light: 800 lumens.",kettle:"Heats the water to 100 \xB0C and then switches itself off.",heater:"Together with the kettle it takes 3.5 kW.",tv:"Takes a steady 100 W while it is on.",computer:"Takes 150 W while it is on.",fridge:"The compressor runs 10 minutes in every half hour.",hairdryer:"Takes 1800 W: almost as much as the kettle.",duration:"The run stops after this many hours of simulated time.",timeScale:"How many minutes of simulated time pass in each second on the screen.",water:"Changing the water amount replaces it with a cold batch; energy already used remains on the meter.",tariff:"The default is an example; the real price depends on the tariff.",efficiency:"The share of the electric energy that heats the water.",voltage:"Georgia and Europe use about 220\u2013230 V, the USA 120 V.",breaker:"The largest current the circuit allows.",timeStep:"Fixed physics clock step; energy is integrated exactly across compressor and thermostat switching."},preset:{hour:"1 hour",evening:"6 hours",day:"A day",slow:"Slow",normal:"Normal",fast:"Fast",usa:"USA, 120 V",georgia:"Georgia, 220 V",europe:"Europe, 230 V",a6:"6 A",a10:"10 A",a16:"16 A",a25:"25 A"},appliance:{led:"LED lamp",bulb:"Bulb",kettle:"Kettle",heater:"Heater",tv:"TV",computer:"Computer",fridge:"Refrigerator",hairdryer:"Hair dryer"},measure:{power:"Power now",current:"Current now",energy:"Energy used",cost:"Cost so far (\u20BE)",hours:"Time that has passed",averagePower:"Average power",demandCurrent:"Current if everything switched on ran at full power",breakerLoad:"Share of the breaker's rating",kettleTemp:"Temperature of the water",boilMinutes:"Time the kettle took to boil",kettleNeeded:"Energy needed to boil the water",trips:"Times the breaker has tripped",breakerPower:"Greatest power the breaker allows",kettleEnergy:"Energy used by the kettle",breaker:"Rating of the breaker"},view:{energy:"Energy used: {energy}",cost:"Cost: {cost} \u20BE",load:"Power {power} \xB7 current {current}",breaker:"Breaker {rating}: limit {limit}",demand:"Everything on would draw {demand}",tripped:"The breaker has tripped! Switch something off.",hint:"Choose appliances, press Play, then switch them on or off",time:"Time: {time}",legend:"The disc of the meter is not to scale: it turns once per 0.5 kWh",off:"off",noPower:"no power",boiled:"boiled, {temperature}",heating:"{power} \xB7 {temperature}",compressorOn:"{power} \xB7 running",compressorOff:"resting"},graph:{power:"Power against time (dotted: what the breaker allows)",energy:"Energy used against time",current:"Current against time (dotted: the breaker's rating)"},challenge:{ledLight:{title:"Light for 9 watts",description:"Light the room with the LED lamp alone, so that the whole home takes no more than 10 W.",hint:"Switch on the LED lamp and leave everything else off."},threeAndHalf:{title:"Three and a half kilowatts",description:"Switch on appliances so that the home takes 3.5 kW (give or take 50 W) without the breaker tripping.",hint:"The kettle takes 2000 W and the heater 1500 W."},overload:{title:"An overloaded circuit",description:"Switch on so many appliances that the current would be above the rating of the breaker.",hint:"The kettle and the heater are just inside the limit: add a lamp."},oneKwh:{title:"One kilowatt-hour",description:"Let the run end with the meter showing 1 kWh (give or take 0.05 kWh).",hint:"E = P\xB7t: for example the TV and the computer (250 W) for 4 hours."},boilFast:{title:"Boil it in two minutes",description:"Boil the water in the kettle in 2 minutes or less, and let the run end.",hint:"Less water boils sooner: t = m\xB7c\xB7\u0394T / (\u03B7\xB7P); try 0.6 kg."},cheapEvening:{title:"A cheap evening",description:"Run for at least 5 hours with the lights on (at least 0.04 kWh), spending no more than 0.02 \u20BE.",hint:"The LED lamp: 9 W for 6 hours is only 0.054 kWh. The bulb costs 6.7 times more."},limit:{title:"Right at the limit",description:"Use 95 % to 100 % of the breaker's rating without tripping it.",hint:"At 220 V and 16 A the limit is 3520 W: the kettle and the heater use 99 % of it."}}}},a={sim:r};export{a as default,r as sim};
